    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Persian, first written in the inscriptions of the Achaemenid kings in the 6th century BCE, has been spoken and written for some two and a half thousand years, in three stages known as Old, Middle and New Persian.</li><li>Reborn after the Arab conquest as New Persian, written in Arabic letters, it became from the 10th century the language of a great literature, from Ferdowsi’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahnameh">Shahnameh</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rumi">Rumi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafez">Hafez</a>, and of courts and learning from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</li><li>The language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and Tajikistan today, it has changed so little since the 10th century that Persian speakers can still read the classical poets with ease.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Persian was the official language of the courts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> until 1837, and words such as bazaar, caravan, pyjamas, paradise and checkmate, from shāh māt, “the king is helpless”, reached English from or through Persian.</p></aside>
    </section>

