    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 520 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> has his deeds carved in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a>, Elamite and Babylonian on the cliff of Behistun</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 262</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i">Shapur I</a> records his victories over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a>, Parthian and Greek at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-rostam">Naqsh-e Rostam</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">651</span><span class="rf-text">The fall of the Sasanian empire to the Arab conquerors makes Arabic the language of government</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">10th century</span><span class="rf-text">The Samanid court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> patronises poetry in the new Persian language</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1010</span><span class="rf-text">Ferdowsi completes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahnameh">Shahnameh</a>, the national epic of the Persian-speaking world</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1273</span><span class="rf-text">Death of the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rumi">Rumi</a>, author of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masnavi">Masnavi</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1582</span><span class="rf-text">The Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> makes Persian the language of all government records in his empire</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1837</span><span class="rf-text">Persian is replaced by English and local languages as the official language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/darius-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/darius-i.jpg" alt="Darius I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Darius_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rumlu, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Persian belongs to the Iranian branch of the Indo-European family, which also includes Avestan, the language of the Zoroastrian scriptures, and Kurdish, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashto">Pashto</a>, Baluchi and Ossetian today. Its name comes from Parsa, the land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, the present-day province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>, from which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid dynasty</a> built the largest empire the world had yet seen in the 6th century BCE.</p><p>The earliest Persian texts are the inscriptions of the Achaemenid kings, written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a>, a script of some three dozen signs for syllables and sounds with a few word signs, probably created at the order of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> for his royal inscriptions. The most famous is the great inscription that Darius had carved around 520 BCE on the cliff of Behistun in western Iran, in Old Persian, Elamite and Babylonian, telling how he had won the throne. The empire itself was administered mainly in Elamite and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>, the common language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a>, and Old Persian was used almost only for royal monuments.</p><p>After the conquest of Alexander, Persian developed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a>, which became the official language of the Sasanian empire from the 3rd century CE. It was written in the Pahlavi script, derived from Aramaic, in which many Aramaic words were written but read as their Persian equivalents. The inscriptions of the Sasanian kings, Zoroastrian scriptures and commentaries, and translations from Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> were written in Middle Persian.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muslim-conquest-of-persia">Arab conquest of Iran</a>, completed by 651, made Arabic the language of religion, government and learning. For two centuries Persian was little written, though it continued to be spoken. In the 9th and 10th centuries, under Iranian dynasties ruling in the east, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, a new written Persian emerged, using the Arabic alphabet and enriched with Arabic words: New Persian, the language of Persian literature ever since.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="structure">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sounds, Script &amp; Grammar</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/shahnameh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shahnameh.jpg" alt="Shahnameh" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/shahnameh">Shahnameh</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Court_of_Gayumars_(Cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Attributed to Sultan Muhammad, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Over its history Persian became one of the simplest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-european-languages">Indo-European languages</a> in its grammar. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a>, like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, had many cases, three genders and complex verb forms; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a> lost most of these endings; and New Persian has no grammatical gender, even in its pronouns, and no case endings, except a suffix, -rā, that marks a definite direct object.</p><p>Instead, Persian links words with the ezāfe, a short vowel joining a noun to the words that describe it, so that ketāb-e bozorg means “the big book”, and the ezāfe can link a chain of words, as in the names of poems and buildings. The usual order of words is subject, object and verb. Many verbs are formed by combining a noun or adjective with a small number of verbs such as “to do” and “to become”, and new verbs are created in this way.</p><p>New Persian is written in the Arabic alphabet, from right to left, with four extra letters for the sounds p, ch, zh and g, which Arabic lacks. Persian scribes developed the elegant Nastaliq script in the 14th and 15th centuries, which became the classic hand of Persian, and later of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">calligraphy</a>. In Tajikistan, Persian has been written in the Cyrillic alphabet since 1940.</p><p>Arabic words form a large part of the Persian vocabulary, especially in learned, religious and abstract language, and Persian has also borrowed from Turkish, French and English. Ferdowsi, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahnameh">Shahnameh</a>, used relatively few Arabic words, and in the 20th century an academy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> coined Persian words to replace foreign ones. Persian is spoken today in three standard forms, the Persian of Iran, Dari in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and Tajik in Tajikistan, which are largely mutually intelligible.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="speakers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Speakers &amp; Spread</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bukhara"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bukhara.jpg" alt="Bukhara" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kalon-Ensemble_Buchara.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Euyasik, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenids</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a>, Persian and its relatives were the languages of the ruling peoples of great empires. After the Arab conquest, New Persian first flourished at the courts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saffarid-dynasty">Saffarids</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a>, who ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> in the 9th and 10th centuries and encouraged poets to write in Persian, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudaki">Rudaki</a>, often called the father of Persian poetry.</p><p>From the 11th century, Persian became the language of government and culture of a vast area, ruled mostly by Turkic and Mongol dynasties: the Ghaznavids, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanids</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurids</a> all used Persian as their language of administration and patronised Persian poets, historians and artists. Persian spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, where the Seljuk sultans of Rum hosted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rumi">Rumi</a>, and Ottoman Turkish absorbed a large Persian vocabulary.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, Persian was the language of government of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> and above all of the Mughal empire, whose emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> made it the language of all government records in 1582. It became the language of administration, diplomacy, history and literature across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>, used by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> as well as Muslims, until the British replaced it as an official language with English and local languages in 1837. Its influence created <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a>, which is written in the Persian form of the Arabic script and contains a large Persian vocabulary.</p><p>Historians speak of a “Persianate world”: for some eight centuries Persian was the shared language of culture from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and Anatolia to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, read and written by people of many mother tongues. Today it is the official language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, an official language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, as Dari, and of Tajikistan, as Tajik, and is spoken by large communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a> and by a worldwide diaspora.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="literature">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Literature &amp; Learning</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/layla-and-majnun"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/layla-and-majnun.jpg" alt="Layla and Majnun" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/layla-and-majnun">Layla and Majnun</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Layla_and_Majnun2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Persian literature is among the richest in the world, and above all a literature of poetry. Its first great work is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahnameh">Shahnameh</a>, the “Book of Kings”, completed by Ferdowsi around 1010 after some thirty years of work: an epic of some 50,000 couplets telling the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> from the creation of the world to the Arab conquest, with the tragedies of heroes such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam">Rostam</a> and Sohrab. Ferdowsi drew on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a> histories, now lost, and his poem became the national epic of the Persian-speaking world.</p><p>In the following centuries Persian poetry reached heights of mystical and lyric expression. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omar-khayyam">Omar Khayyam</a>, also a great mathematician and astronomer, wrote quatrains on the brevity of life; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizami-ganjavi">Nizami</a> wrote romantic epics such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/layla-and-majnun">Layla and Majnun</a> and Khosrow and Shirin; Attar wrote the Conference of the Birds, an allegory of the mystical path; Saadi wrote the Gulistan, “The Rose Garden”, in prose and verse; and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rumi">Rumi</a>, who died in 1273, composed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masnavi">Masnavi</a>, an immense poem of mystical teaching, and thousands of lyric poems. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafez">Hafez</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>, who died around 1390, is regarded by many Iranians as the greatest of their poets, and copies of his poems are found in Iranian homes, where they are opened for guidance.</p><p>Persian prose includes histories, such as the world history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-al-din-hamadani">Rashid al-Din</a> in the 14th century, travel accounts, mirrors for princes, and the memoirs of rulers; Middle Persian had already transmitted to the Islamic world the Indian fables of the Panchatantra, as Kalila and Dimna.</p><p>In the modern period, writers such as Sadegh Hedayat, whose novel The Blind Owl appeared in 1937, the poet Nima Yushij, who created modern Persian verse, and Forough Farrokhzad renewed Persian literature. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, Goethe’s West-Eastern Divan was inspired by Hafez, and Edward FitzGerald’s free English version of the Rubaiyat of Omar Khayyam, of 1859, became one of the most popular poems in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-language">English language</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="change">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Change &amp; Descendants</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hafez"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hafez.jpg" alt="Hafez" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hafez">Hafez</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anselm_Feuerbach_Hafis_vor_der_Schenke_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anselm Feuerbach, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Persian has changed remarkably little since the 10th century. A modern reader of Persian can read Ferdowsi or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafez">Hafez</a> with far less difficulty than an English speaker reading Chaucer, although pronunciation and vocabulary have changed, and the Persian spoken in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dushanbe">Dushanbe</a> has developed its own features.</p><p>The modern standard developed in the 19th and 20th centuries, as newspapers, schools, radio and a modern literature took shape. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, an academy of the Persian language was founded in 1935 to coin new Persian words for modern concepts, a task continued by its successor today. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, Tajik was written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> letters from 1928 and in Cyrillic from 1940, which separated it in writing from the Persian of Iran and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>.</p><p>Persian has lent words to many languages: to Turkish, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> and Hindi, to Bengali and Punjabi, to Armenian and Georgian, to the Turkic languages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and, often through them, to Arabic, Russian and English.</p><p>Words such as bazaar, caravan, khaki, pyjamas, shawl and jasmine reached English from Persian, often through Hindustani, and paradise comes, through Greek, from an Old Iranian word for a walled garden. The game of chess reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, and with it the words check and checkmate, from shāh, “king”.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rumi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rumi.jpg" alt="Rumi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rumi">Rumi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D9%85%D9%88%D9%84%D8%A7%D9%86%D8%A7_%D8%A7%D8%AB%D8%B1_%D8%AD%D8%B3%DB%8C%D9%86_%D8%A8%D9%87%D8%B2%D8%A7%D8%AF_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hossein Behzad, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Persian has been one of the great languages of civilisation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. For eight centuries it carried literature, history, science, administration and diplomacy across a vast area, from the Ottoman court to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, and created a shared culture among peoples of many languages and religions.</p><p>Its poetry is one of the treasures of world literature. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rumi">Rumi</a> is among the most widely read poets in the world in translation, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafez">Hafez</a> and Saadi are quoted in daily conversation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, and verses of Saadi on the unity of humankind are often quoted in connection with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>. Iranians celebrate the Persian New Year, Nowruz, and the long night of Yalda with readings from Hafez and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahnameh">Shahnameh</a>.</p><p>Persian also shaped the arts of the Islamic world: Nastaliq <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">calligraphy</a>, the illustrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of the Shahnameh and other poems, and the inscriptions of buildings across Iran, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><p>Today Persian is the language of a living literature, cinema and music in Iran, Afghanistan and Tajikistan and in the diaspora, and its continuity over two and a half thousand years makes it one of the most enduring languages of culture in the world.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Persian, the Iranian language of the Achaemenid kings, the Sasanian empire and, reborn after the Arab conquest, of Ferdowsi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rumi">Rumi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafez">Hafez</a>, became the language of courts and culture from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Spoken and written for some two and a half thousand years, it remains the language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and Tajikistan, and one of the great literary languages of the world.</em></p>

